      "text": "A curve is given in polar coordinates by \\( r = 3 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 9 \\), where \\( x = r\\cos\\theta \\) and \\( y = r\\sin\\theta \\). At a point P on the curve, \\( \\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} + 1 \\) and \\( \\frac{dy}{d\\theta} = - \\frac{9 \\sqrt{2}}{2} - 3 \\). A calculator may be used. Without differentiating the polar equation, find the exact value of \\( \\frac{dx}{d\\theta} \\) at P, and show the work that leads to your answer.",
